  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Thursday)\n\nWeather Pattern Overview: The upper-level ridge centered over the\nRocky Mountains will continue to dominate the local weather \ntoday, keeping warm and dry conditions in place with highs as much\nas 5-10 degrees above normal for this time of year. Winds will \ngenerally remain light, though will become breezy across portions \nof central Nevada this afternoon with gusts up to 25-30 mph, \nespecially in northwestern Nye and central Eureka counties.\n\nWindy conditions will begin to overspread much of the rest of\nnorthern and central Nevada by late tonight and into Friday as an\nupper- level trough and associated low pressure system moves into\nthe western CONUS. Southerly winds will increase ahead of this \nsystem, with sustained winds 20-25 mph and gusts as high as 45 \nmph. Cannot rule out a few gusts up to 50 mph, especially in \ncentral Nevada. Moisture will begin to increase across the area \nunder this southerly flow Friday afternoon with rain showers \nincreasing in coverage and intensity into the overnight hours \nFriday. Snow levels will start out above 8,000 feet, dropping to \nas low as 6,000 feet Saturday morning as cooler air filters in. \nOnly light snowfall accumulations are expected and should remain \nconfined to high elevation summits and passes. This system should \nbe quick moving, with any lingering precipitation exiting the \nregion by mid-morning Saturday. Here are the probabilities of \nsummits receiving a half inch or more of snow over 24 hours:\n\nAdobe Summit - 30%\nEmigrant Pass - 20%\nSecret Pass \u2013 82%\nPequop Summit \u2013 20%\nAustin Summit - 55%\nBob Scotts Summit - 90%\nGarden Pass - 70%\nHickison Summit - 10%\nPinto Summit - 75%\nConnors Pass - 65%\nMurry Summit - 60%\nRobinson Summit - 75%\n\nWeak ridging will briefly build back over the Silver State in the\nwake of this system Sunday before another trough of low pressure\nenters the area Monday. There remains some uncertainty in guidance\nregarding the timing of this system and associated precipitation\nlate Monday into Tuesday. Regardless, slightly cooler air aloft\n(compared to our Friday system) may result in a \u201ccolder\u201d system.\nPeriods of light rain may transition to snow showers as the system\ndeparts Tuesday morning, even in area valleys across northern and\ncentral Nevada. Active pattern is expected to persist into mid-\nweek with yet another possible area of low pressure over the\nEastern Pacific progged to impact the western CONUS Wednesday and\nThursday. Highs near normal this weekend will trend below normal\nby mid-week next week, into the upper 30s to middle 40s. Lows will\nremain in the 20s for most.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence for above normal\ntemperatures and dry, calm weather today. High confidence for high\nwinds tomorrow across northern and central Nevada. High confidence\nfor precipitation and mountain snow tomorrow night into Saturday\nmorning with low to moderate confidence of snow reaching the\nhigher passes and summits. Moderate confidence of a second weather\nsystem impacting Nevada Monday through Tuesday. Low confidence of\na third weather system impacting Nevada next Wednesday through \nnext Thursday. No changes to the weather grids at this time.",
